.SH NAME
ktm-guide-pointers \- Pointers (chapter 8 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A pointer holds the address of a value. This chapter covers taking addresses, reading and writing through pointers, pointers to structs, \fBnull\fR, pointer arithmetic, and the conversions between pointer types. For a pointer that knows its length, see Arrays and slices (see ktm-guide-arrays-and-slices(7)).
.SH Address and dereference
.PP
\fB&x\fR is the address of \fBx\fR; its type is \fBT *\fR for an \fBx\fR of type \fBT\fR\&. \fB*p\fR is the value \fBp\fR points to, for reading or writing.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 x = 5;
    i64 *p = &x;
    printf("%d\en", *p);
    *p = 10;
    printf("%d %d\en", x, *p);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
5
10 10
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two pointers to the same variable see the same value:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 score = 1;
    i64 *a = &score;
    i64 *b = &score;
    *a += 10;
    *b *= 2;
    printf("%d %d\en", score, a == b);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
22 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
A pointer to a pointer works the same way, one level at a time:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 x = 1;
    i64 *p = &x;
    i64 **pp = &p;
    **pp = 20;
    printf("%d\en", x);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
20
.EE
.RE
.SH Pointers as parameters
.PP
Arguments are copies, so a function that should change a caller's variable takes its address.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void swap(i64 *a, i64 *b) {
    i64 t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

void main() {
    i64 x = 1, y = 2;
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\en", x, y);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
2 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
It is also how a function reads a big struct without copying it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Stats { i64 count; i64 sum; i64 min; i64 max; };

void add(Stats *s, i64 v) {
    if (s.count == 0 || v < s.min) { s.min = v; }
    if (s.count == 0 || v > s.max) { s.max = v; }
    s.count++;
    s.sum += v;
}

void main() {
    Stats s;
    i64 values[] = {7, 3, 9, 4};
    for (auto v : values) {
        add(&s, v);
    }
    printf("%d values, sum %d, %d..%d\en", s.count, s.sum, s.min, s.max);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
4 values, sum 23, 3..9
.EE
.RE
.SH Pointers to structs
.PP
\fB\&.\fR works through a pointer: on a \fBPoint *p\fR, \fBp.x\fR is the field \fBx\fR of the struct \fBp\fR points to. C's \fBp\->x\fR means the same and works too.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x; i64 y; };

void main() {
    Point a = {1, 2};
    Point *p = &a;
    p.x = 11;
    p\->y = 22;
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", a.x, a.y, p.x, (*p).y);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
11 22 11 22
.EE
.RE
.PP
The automatic dereference goes one level only. On a \fBPoint **pp\fR, write \fB(*pp).x\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x; i64 y; };

void reset(Point **pp) {
    (*pp).x = 0;
}

void main() {
    Point a = {5, 6};
    Point *p = &a;
    reset(&p);
    printf("%d %d\en", a.x, a.y);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 6
.EE
.RE
.SH null
.PP
\fBnull\fR is the pointer to nothing. A pointer declared without a value starts as \fBnull\fR, and a pointer is false in a condition when it is \fBnull\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Node { i64 value; Node *next; };

void main() {
    Node c = {3, null};
    Node b = {2, &c};
    Node a = {1, &b};
    for (Node *n = &a; n; n = n.next) {
        printf("%d ", n.value);
    }
    printf("\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 3 
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reading or writing through \fBnull\fR stops the program with a crash report that names the function and line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
struct Point { i64 x; i64 y; };

i64 getX(Point *p) {
    return p.x;
}

void main() {
    Point *nothing = null;
    printf("before\en");
    printf("%d\en", getX(nothing));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
before
crash: null pointer dereference (address 0x0)
  at example.ktm:4 in getX (inlined)
  called from example.ktm:10 in main
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fB(inlined)\fR says the compiler copied \fBgetX\fR into \fBmain\fR instead of calling it; the report still shows it as its own step. Safety and debugging (see ktm-guide-safety-and-debugging(7)) covers crash reports in full.
.SH Pointer arithmetic
.PP
Adding \fBn\fR to a pointer moves it \fBn\fR elements forward (not \fBn\fR bytes). \fBp[i]\fR is \fB*(p + i)\fR\&. Subtracting two pointers gives the number of elements between them.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 nums[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};
    i64 *p = nums;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", *p, *(p + 2), p[3]);
    i64 *last = &nums[3];
    printf("%d elements apart\en", last \- p);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
10 30 40
3 elements apart
.EE
.RE
.PP
Walking a C string with a pointer:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 *s = "hello";
    u8 *end = s;
    while (*end) {
        end++;
    }
    printf("%d bytes, the last is %c\en", end \- s, *(end \- 1));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
5 bytes, the last is o
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBn + p\fR is the same as \fBp + n\fR; \fBn \- p\fR is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB++\fR and \fB\-\-\fR on a pointer move it one element.
.IP \(bu 2
A pointer compares with another pointer, or with \fBnull\fR\&.
.SH Converting between pointer types
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvoid *\fR and the byte pointers (\fBu8 *\fR, \fBi8 *\fR, \fBbool *\fR) convert to and from any pointer without a cast.
.IP \(bu 2
A pointer to a struct converts to a pointer to the struct it extends (see Structs (see ktm-guide-structs-and-unions(7))).
.IP \(bu 2
Any other conversion between pointer types needs a cast.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    f64 f = 1.5;
    void *any = &f;
    f64 *back = any;
    u8 *bytes = &f;
    i64 *bits = (i64 *)&f;
    printf("%.1f %d %x\en", *back, bytes[7], *bits);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1.5 63 3ff8000000000000
.EE
.RE
.PP
A \fBvoid *\fR is how a function takes "a pointer to anything", together with what it needs to know about it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void dump(void *data, i64 size) {
    u8 *b = data;
    for (i64 i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        printf("%02x ", b[i]);
    }
    printf("\en");
}

void main() {
    i32 n = 0x11223344;
    u16 small = 0xABCD;
    dump(&n, sizeof(n));
    dump(&small, sizeof(small));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
44 33 22 11 
cd ab 
.EE
.RE
.SH Pointers that outlive their target
.PP
A local lives until its function returns. A pointer to it must not be used after that: the memory is reused by the next call. Return the value itself, or memory from the heap (Memory (see ktm-guide-memory(7))).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
i64 *wrong() {
    i64 local = 42;
    return &local;     // the address of a local that ends here
}

i64 right() {
    i64 local = 42;
    return local;      // a copy of the value
}

void main() {
    printf("%d\en", right());
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42
.EE
.RE
